course: dictionary
lesson: 5219
title: Jeans, denim and names
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word jeans means trousers made of strong blue cotton called denim. Look at the ending: jeans has an s, because trousers have two legs, so one pair is still called «a pair of jeans».
explain: Jean can also describe something made of denim, as in «a jean jacket». Here jean is working like a describing word, telling you what the jacket is made of. Ask yourself: is the word naming a thing, or describing one?
explain: Jean is also a name. In English, Jean is usually a woman's name and rhymes with «seen». In French, Jean is a man's name, said «zhahn», like John. The words around a name, such as «scientist» or «novel», give you clues about who is meant.
fact: Jeans is a plural word, so we say «a pair of jeans», not «a jeans».
fact: Denim is the strong cotton cloth that jeans are usually made from.
fact: «Jean» can be a noun, an adjective in phrases like «a jean jacket», or a name.
fact: In 1958 the scientist «Jean Berko Gleason» made the wug test, which showed that children know grammar rules.
fact: In French, «un pantalon» means one pair of trousers, and it is singular, like «un jean».
exercise: «Ravi wore a pair of blue jeans to school on non-uniform day.» What are jeans? | A kind of jacket with a hood | *Trousers made of strong blue cotton called denim | Shoes for running fast | A type of bread
exercise: «She bought a jean jacket with big silver buttons.» What does jean mean in «jean jacket»? | A name for a girl | Waterproof | Very old | *Made of denim
exercise: Mia asks, «Is Jean a girl's name or a boy's name?» What is the best answer? | It is a type of trousers only | It is a number | *In English it is usually a woman's name, but in French it is a man's name | It is always a boy's name
test: «The scientist Jean Berko Gleason made the wug test in 1958.» In this sentence, what kind of word is Jean? | Trousers | A word meaning old | *A name for a person | A type of cloth
test: Why does the word jeans end in s, even when you mean just one pair? | *Because it is plural: the trousers have two legs | Because it ends in a name | Because it is a French word
exam: Ella says, «I bought a jean for school.» What is the best correction? | I bought jean for school, it is a trousers | I bought a Jean for school, it is a name | *I bought a pair of jeans for school | I bought a jeans for school
